      "notes": "All claims are well-supported by quoted evidence at the correct tier. The measurable claims (E7, E8, E9, E13, E20, E22, E24, E31) are factual baselines from credible sources, not projections. The projected claim (E15) is correctly tiered and uses appropriately hedged language ('might', 'could'). The mixed direction is genuinely supported by claims on both sides: positive (dedicated court division improving enforcement) and negative (50/50 mandate risking litigation surge). No lever leakage \u2014 the verdict is scored on justice system functioning, not on the policy merits per se. No party identity or motive language detected. Magnitude 'minor' and time horizon 'this-parliament' are reasonable given the low confidence and absence of institutional modelling. One minor note: E24 (high conflict/stress claim) is sourced from a solicitors firm (trethowans.com) which is effectively a commercial/advocacy source, but it is used only as a supporting claim and is consistent with well-established family law jurisprudence, so this does not rise to blocking. Similarly, E7 and E9 lean on opfs.org.uk (advocacy) but are corroborated by other sources and used as illustrative baselines rather than sole verdict drivers."
